In acute triangle $\triangle ABC$, the sides opposite to angles $A$, $B$, and $C$ are $a$, $b$, and $c$ respectively. Given that $a=\sqrt{7}$, $b=3$, and $\sqrt{7}\sin B+\sin A=2\sqrt{3}$.

(I) Find the measure of angle $A$;

(II) Find the area of $\triangle ABC$.

Find $\tan X$ in the right triangle shown below.

[asy]
pair A, B, X;
A = (0,0);
X = (12,0);
B = (0,5);
draw(B--X--A--B);
draw(rightanglemark(B,A,X,20));
label("$A$", A, SW);
label("$X$", X, SE);
label("$B$", B, N);
label("$13$", (B+X)/2, NE);
label("$12$", X/2, S);
[/asy]

Given the function $f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c$ ($a \neq 0$), and the equation $f(x) = x$ has no real roots, consider the following statements:  
① The equation $f[f(x)] = x$ also has no real roots;  
② If $a < 0$, then there must exist a real number $x$ such that $f[f(x)] > x$;  
③ If $a > 0$, then the inequality $f[f(x)] > x$ holds for all real numbers $x$;  
④ If $a + b + c = 0$, then the inequality $f[f(x)] < x$ holds for all real numbers $x$;  
Among the above statements, the correct ones are: (Fill in all the correct statement numbers).

Let the function $f(x) = |x + \frac{8}{m}| + |x - 2m|$ where $m > 0$.

(Ⅰ) Prove that $f(x) \geq 8$ always holds;  
(Ⅱ) Find the range of real numbers $m$ for which the inequality $f(1) > 10$ holds.

Given the function $f(x) = x^2 + ax + 2$;  
(1) When $a=-1$, determine the intervals of monotonicity for the function $f(x)$.  
(2) If the function $f(x)$ is a monotonic function on the interval $\[-5, 5\]$, find the range of possible values for $a$.

There are 20 products among which 5 are defective, and the rest are up to standard. Two products are drawn one after the other without replacement. Calculate the probability of:
(1) Drawing a defective product on the first draw;
(2) Drawing a defective product on both the first and the second draws;
(3) Drawing a defective product on the second draw, given that the first product drawn was defective.

Let $S_{n}$ be the sum of the first $n$ terms of the positive sequence ${a_{n}}$, and $S_{n}= \frac{1}{4}a_{n}^{2}+ \frac{1}{2}a_{n}- \frac{3}{4}$.
(1) Find the value of $a_{1}$;
(2) Find the general term formula for the sequence ${a_{n}}$;
(3) Given $b_{n}=2^{n}$, find the value of $T_{n}=a_{1}b_{1}+a_{2}b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n}b_{n}$.

How many continuous paths from $P$ to $Q$, along segments of the figure, do not revisit any of the six labeled points?

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--(0,0)--cycle,linewidth(2));
draw((0,3)--(1.5,0)--(3,3)--(0,3)--cycle,linewidth(2));
draw((0,3)--(1.5,4.5)--(3,3),linewidth(2));

label("$P$",(1.5,4.5),N);
label("$Q$",(0,0),SW);
label("$R$",(0,3),W);
label("$S$",(3,3),E);
label("$T$",(3,0),SE);
label("$U$",(1.5,0),S);
[/asy]

A function $g$ from the integers to the integers is defined as follows:
\[g(n) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
n + 5 & \text{if $n$ is odd}, \\
n/2 & \text{if $n$ is even}.
\end{array}
\right.\]
Suppose $m$ is odd and $g(g(g(m))) = 65.$ Find $m.$

Given the parabola: $y^{2}=2px (p > 0)$, its focus is $F$, and point $A(m,2) (m > 1)$ is a point on the parabola, satisfying $|AF|= \frac {5}{2}$.
(1) Find the equation of the parabola;
(2) Given $M(-2,0)$, $N(2,0)$, the line passing through $N$ intersects the parabola at points $C$ and $D$. If $S_{\triangle MCD}=16$, find the equation of the line $CD$.

Which of the following statements is correct? _______.

(1) If $(2x-1)+i=y-(3-y)i$, where $x\in \mathbb{R}$, $y\in \mathbb{C}\backslash \mathbb{R}$, then it must be $\begin{cases} & 2x-1=y \\ & 1=-(3-y) \end{cases}$;

(2) $2+i > 1+i$;

(3) The points on the imaginary axis represent purely imaginary numbers;

(4) If a number is real, then it has no imaginary part;

(5) If $z= \frac{1}{i}$, then $z^{3}+1$ corresponds to a point in the first quadrant of the complex plane.

What is the number of units in the area of the circle with center at \( R \) and passing through \( S \)? Express your answer in terms of \( \pi \).

[asy]
size(150); pair R = (1,2), S=(-7,6); string stringpair(pair p){return "$("+string(p.x)+", "+string(p.y)+"$)";}
draw((-15,0)--(15,0),Arrows(4)); draw((0,-15)--(0,15),Arrows(4));
dot("$S$"+stringpair(S),S,SE,linewidth(3)); dot("$R$"+stringpair(R),R,NW,linewidth(3));
[/asy]

Given the function $f(x)=\sin x+a\cos x$, whose graph passes through the point $(\frac{\pi}{3},0)$.
(1) Find the value of the real number $a$;
(2) If $g(x)=[f(x)]^{2}-2$, find the range of the function $g(x)$ when $x\in(\frac{\pi}{4},\frac{2\pi}{3})$;
(3) If $g(\frac{a}{2})=-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$ $(\frac{\pi}{6} < a < \frac{2\pi}{3})$, find the value of $\cos(\alpha+\frac{3\pi}{2})$.

In the set of integers \\(Z\\), all integers that have a remainder of \\(r\\) when divided by \\(7\\) form a "class", denoted as \\(\[r\]\\), i.e., \\(\[r\]=\\{7k+r|k∈Z\\}\\), where \\(r=0\\), \\(1\\), \\(2\\), \\(…6.\\) The following five statements are given:  
\\(①2016∈\[1\]\\);  
\\(②-3∈\[4\]\\);  
\\(③\[3\]∩\[6\]=Ø\\);  
\\(④Z=\[0\]∪\[1\]∪\[2\]∪\[3\]∪\[4\]∪\[5\]∪\[6\]\\);  
\\(⑤\\) "The necessary and sufficient condition for integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) to belong to the same 'class'" is "if and only if \\(a-b∈\[0\]\\)."  
Among these, the number of correct statements is (　　)  
A: \\(5\\)  
B: \\(4\\)  
C: \\(3\\)  
D: \\(2\\)

In this square array of 25 dots (5x5), four dots are to be chosen at random. What is the probability that the four dots will be collinear? Express your answer as a common fraction.

[asy]
size(70);
for(int i = 0; i<5; ++i)
for(int j = 0; j<5; ++j)
dot((i,j),linewidth(5));
[/asy]

Given $f_n(x) = \binom{n}{0}x^n - \binom{n}{1}(x-1)^n + \ldots + (-1)^k\binom{n}{k}(x-k)^n + \ldots + (-1)^n\binom{n}{n}(x-n)^n$, where $x \in \mathbb{R}$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$, $k \in \mathbb{N}$, $k \leq n$.

(1) Find the values of $f_1(x)$, $f_2(x)$, and $f_3(x)$;  
(2) Guess the expression of $f_n(x)$ with respect to $n$ and prove your conjecture.

Let \\(f(x)=e^{x}-x-1\\) and \\(g(x)=e^{x}(ax+x\cos x+1)\\).  
\\((1)\\) Find the extreme values of the function \\(f(x)\\);  
\\((2)\\) Prove that if \\(a > -1\\), then \\(g(x) > 1\\) for \\(x∈(0,1)\\).

Given the function $f(x) = 2\sin(\omega x)$, where the constant $\omega > 0$,
(1) If $y = f(x)$ is monotonically increasing on the interval $\left[-\frac{3\pi}{4}, \frac{\pi}{3}\right]$, find the range of $\omega$;
(2) Let $\omega = 2$, shift the graph of the function $y = f(x)$ to the left by $\frac{\pi}{6}$ units and then shift upward by 1 unit to obtain the graph of the function $y = g(x)$. For the interval $[a, b]$ ($a < b$, $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$) to satisfy: $y = g(x)$ has at least 20 zeros on $[a, b]$. Among all such intervals $[a, b]$ that satisfy the condition, find the minimum value of $b-a$.

Part of the graph of  $ f(x) \equal{} x^3 \plus{} bx^2 \plus{} cx \plus{} d$  is shown. What is  $ b$ ?
[asy]import graph;
unitsize(1.5cm);
defaultpen(linewidth(.8pt)+fontsize(8pt));
dotfactor=3;

real y(real x)
{
return (x-1)*(x+1)*(x-2);
}

path bounds=(-1.5,-1)--(1.5,-1)--(1.5,2.5)--(-1.5,2.5)--cycle;

pair[] points={(-1,0),(0,2),(1,0)};
draw(bounds,white);
draw(graph(y,-1.5,1.5));
drawline((0,0),(1,0));
drawline((0,0),(0,1));
dot(points);
label(" $(-1,0)$ ",(-1,0),SE);
label(" $(1,0)$ ",(1,0),SW);
label(" $(0,2)$ ",(0,2),NE);

clip(bounds);[/asy] $ \textbf{(A)}\minus{}\!4 \qquad \textbf{(B)}\minus{}\!2 \qquad \textbf{(C)}\ 0 \qquad \textbf{(D)}\ 2 \qquad \textbf{(E)}\ 4$

Given the function $f(x)=\ln (x-1)-k(x-1)+1$.
$(1)$ When $k=1$, find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$;
$(2)$ If the function $f(x)$ has no zeros, find the range of the real number $k$.

Given the function $f(x)=e^{x}-a(x-1)$ $(a∈R)$.
$(1)$ Find the monotonic intervals of the function $f(x)$;
$(2)$ If $m$, $n$, $p$ satisfy $|m-p| < |n-p|$ always holds, then $m$ is closer to $p$ than $n$. Under the condition that the function $f(x)$ has an extreme value, when $x≥1$, $\frac {e}{x}$ is closer to $\ln x$ than $e^{x-1}+a$, try to find the range of $a$.

A region $S$ in the complex plane is defined by \begin{align*}
S = \{x + iy: - 1\le x\le1, - 1\le y\le1\}.
\end{align*}
A complex number $z = x + iy$ is chosen uniformly at random from $S$. What is the probability that $\left(\frac12 + \frac12i\right)z$ is also in $S$?

Functions \( f_{0}, f_{1}, f_{2}, \cdots \) are recursively defined as follows:
1. \( f_{0}(x) = x, x \in \mathbf{R} \);
2. \( f_{2k+1}(x) = 3^{f_{k}(x)}, x \in \mathbf{R}, k = 0, 1, 2, \cdots \);
3. \( f_{2k}(x) = 2^{f_{k-1}(x)}, x \in \mathbf{R}, k = 1, 2, \cdots \).

Which is larger, \( f_{10}(1) \) or \( f_{9}(2) \)? Prove your conclusion.

Given vectors $\overrightarrow{a}=(\sin θ,-2)$ and $\overrightarrow{b}=(1,\cos θ)$ are orthogonal to each other, where $θ∈(0, \dfrac {π}{2})$:
(1) Find the value of $\tan 2θ$;
(2) If $\sin (θ-φ)= \dfrac { \sqrt {10}}{10}, 0 < φ < \dfrac {π}{2}$, find the value of $\cos φ$.

Find an ordered pair \((x, y)\) that satisfies both of the following equations: 
\begin{align*}
3x - 4y &= -7, \\
4x + 5y &= 23.
\end{align*}

Let {a_n} be a sequence of positive numbers with a partial sum of S_n satisfying the equality 4S_n = a_{n+1}^2 - 4n - 1 for every n ∈ N^*. Suppose that a_2, a_5, and a_14 are precisely the first three terms of a geometric sequence {b_n}.
  (Ⅰ) Prove that the sequence {a_n} is an arithmetic sequence;
  (Ⅱ) Find the sum of the first n terms T_n of the sequence {a_n * b_n}.

Given the function $f(x)=x^{3}+bx^{2}+cx+d$ whose graph passes through point $P(0,1)$, and the equation of the tangent line at point $M(1,f(1))$ is $2x-y-5=0$.  
$(1)$ Find the expression for the function $f(x)$;  
$(2)$ Determine the intervals of monotonicity for the function $y=f(x)$.

Given the universal set $U=\mathbb{R}$, set $A=\{x|x^{2}-2x-3 > 0\}$, and set $B=\{x|4-x^{2}\leqslant 0\}$, find:
$(1) A \cap B$;
$(2) (\lnot_{U}A) \cup (\lnot_{U}B)$.

In triangle $ABC$, the sides opposite to angles $A$, $B$, and $C$ are denoted as $a$, $b$, and $c$, respectively. It is given that $3a\cos C=2c\cos A$ and $\tan C= \frac {1}{2}$.

(Ⅰ) Find $B$;  
(Ⅱ) If $b=5$, find the area of $\triangle ABC$.

In this expanded Number Wall, you add the numbers next to each other and write the sum in the block directly above the two numbers. Which number will be in the block labeled '$x$'? Modify the original layout to have four numbers at the base. [asy]
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,2)--(0,2)--cycle);
draw((2,0)--(2,2));
draw((4,0)--(4,2));
draw((6,0)--(6,2));
draw((8,0)--(8,2));
draw((1,2)--(9,2)--(9,4)--(1,4)--cycle);
draw((3,2)--(3,4));
draw((5,2)--(5,4));
draw((7,2)--(7,4));
draw((2,4)--(2,6)--(8,6)--(8,4)--cycle);
draw((4,4)--(4,6));
draw((6,4)--(6,6));
draw((3,6)--(3,8)--(7,8)--(7,6));
draw((5,6)--(5,8));
label("$x$",(1,1));
label("4",(3,1));
label("8",(5,1));
label("7",(7,1));
label("11",(9,1));
label("$x+4$",(2,3));
label("12",(4,3));
label("15",(6,3));
label("18",(8,3));
label("",(3,5));  // This would be filled in the solution
label("",(5,5));
label("",(7,5));
label("",(5,7));  // This would be the top block with a number calculated based on x
[/asy]

Given vectors $\overrightarrow{a}=(1,-2)$ and $\overrightarrow{b}=(3,4)$:  
$(1)$ If $(3 \overrightarrow{a}- \overrightarrow{b})\parallel(\overrightarrow{a}+k \overrightarrow{b})$, find the value of the real number $k$;  
$(2)$ If $\overrightarrow{a}\perp(m \overrightarrow{a}- \overrightarrow{b})$, find the value of the real number $m$.

Given the hyperbola equation $$\frac {x^{2}}{a^{2}}- \frac {y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1(a>0,b>0)$$, an ellipse C has its vertices at the foci of this hyperbola, and its foci at the vertices of the hyperbola.  
(1) When $$a= \sqrt {3}$$ and $b=1$, find the equation of ellipse C;  
(2) Under the condition of (1), the line $l: y=kx+ \frac {1}{2}$ intersects the y-axis at point P and intersects the ellipse at points A and B. If O is the origin, and the area ratio of $\triangle AOP$ to $\triangle BOP$ is 2:1, find the equation of line $l$;  
(3) If $a=1$, the ellipse C and the line $l': y=x+5$ have common points, find the minimum length of the major axis of this ellipse.

Given that $α \in (0, \frac{π}{2})$ and $\sin (\frac{π}{4} - α) = \frac{\sqrt{10}}{10}$,
(1) find the value of $\tan 2α$;
(2) find the value of $\frac{\sin (α + \frac{π}{4})}{\sin 2α + \cos 2α + 1}$.

In the diagram below, if the area of $\triangle ABC$ is 36, what is the value of $p$? [asy]
size(5cm);defaultpen(fontsize(9));
pair o = (0, 0); pair q = (0, 15); pair b = (15, 0);
pair a = (3, 15); pair t = (3, 0); pair c = (0, p);

draw((-2, 0)--(18, 0), Arrow);
draw((0, -2)--(0, 18), Arrow);
draw(q--a--b);
draw(a--c--b);

label("$Q(0, 15)$", q, W);
label("$A(3, 15)$", a, NE);
label("$B(15, 0)$", b, S);
label("$O(0, 0)$", o, SW);
label("$x$", (18, 0), E);
label("$y$", (0, 18), N);
label("$C(0, p)$", c, W);
[/asy]

In the polar coordinate system, the polar equation of circle $C$ is $\rho =6\cos \theta +8\sin \theta$. Now, establish a Cartesian coordinate system with the pole $O$ as the origin and the positive half-axis of the polar axis as the $x$-axis.  
(Ⅰ) Find the Cartesian coordinate equation of circle $C$;  
(Ⅱ) If the Cartesian coordinates of a moving point $P$ on circle $C$ are $(x,y)$, find the maximum value of $x+y$, and write down the Cartesian coordinates of point $P$ when $x+y$ reaches its maximum value.

Circles $A$, $B$, and $C$ are externally tangent to each other. Circle $A$ has a radius of 2. Circle $B$ has a radius of 1, and is externally tangent to an additional circle $E$, which also touches circle $A$ externally. All circles are in the same plane. Find the radius of circle $C$.

[asy]
unitsize(1cm);
pair A,B,C,E;
A=(0,0);
B=(3,0);
C=(1.5, 1.3228756555);
E=(-1,0);
draw(Circle(A,2),linewidth(0.7));
draw(Circle(B,1),linewidth(0.7));
draw(Circle(C,0.5),linewidth(0.7));
draw(Circle(E,1),linewidth(0.7));
label("$A$",A,W);
label("$B$",B,E);
label("$C$",C,N);
label("$E$",E,W);
[/asy]

In $\triangle ABC$, the sides opposite to angles $A$, $B$, $C$ are $a$, $b$, $c$ respectively, and $a^{2}+b^{2}=ab+c^{2}$.
(I) Find the value of $\tan (C- \dfrac {\pi}{4})$;
(II) If $c= \sqrt {3}$, find the maximum value of $S_{\triangle ABC}$.

In the Cartesian coordinate system $xOy$, a line segment of length $\sqrt{2}+1$ has its endpoints $C$ and $D$ sliding on the $x$-axis and $y$-axis, respectively. It is given that $\overrightarrow{CP} = \sqrt{2} \overrightarrow{PD}$. Let the trajectory of point $P$ be curve $E$.

(I) Find the equation of curve $E$;

(II) A line $l$ passing through point $(0,1)$ intersects curve $E$ at points $A$ and $B$, and $\overrightarrow{OM} = \overrightarrow{OA} + \overrightarrow{OB}$. When point $M$ is on curve $E$, find the area of quadrilateral $OAMB$.

Given that function $f(x)$ is an odd function defined on $\mathbb{R}$, and when $x > 0$, $f(x)=\log_{2}x$.
(1) Find the analytical expression of $f(x)$;
(2) Solve the inequality $f(x)\leqslant \dfrac {1}{2}$ with respect to $x$.

There are the following four propositions:  
(1) The negation of "If $x^2+y^2=0$, then $xy=0$";  
(2) The contrapositive of "If $x>y$, then $x^2>y^2$";  
(3) The negation of "If $x\leq3$, then $x^2-x-6>0$";  
(4) The converse of "Vertical angles are equal".  
Among these, the number of true propositions is (　　)  
A: 0  
B: 1  
C: 2  
D: 3

In $\triangle ABC$, the sides opposite to angles $A$, $B$, and $C$ are denoted as $a$, $b$, and $c$ respectively, and $\tan C= \frac {\sin A+\sin B}{\cos A+\cos B}$.
$(1)$ Find the size of angle $C$;
$(2)$ If the diameter of the circumcircle of $\triangle ABC$ is $1$, find the range of values for $a^{2}+b^{2}$.

Consider the function $f\left(x\right)=4\sin \left(2x+\frac{π}{3}\right)\left(x\in R\right)$. Among the following statements, the correct one(s) is/are:

$\left(①\right)$ The expression of $y=f\left(x\right)$ can be rewritten as $y=4\cos \left(2x-\frac{π}{6}\right)$;

$\left(②\right)$ $y=f\left(x\right)$ is a periodic function with the smallest positive period of $2π$;

$\left(③\right)$ The graph of $y=f\left(x\right)$ is symmetric about the point $\left(-\frac{π}{6},0\right)$;

$\left(④\right)$ The graph of $y=f\left(x\right)$ is symmetric about the line $x=\frac{5π}{6}$.

Let $S\_n$ be the sum of the first $n$ terms of the arithmetic sequence ${a\_n}$, with $a\_1 = 1$ and $S\_7 = 28$. Define $b\_n = [\log_{10} a\_n]$, where $[x]$ denotes the largest integer not greater than $x$ (e.g., $[0.9] = 0$, $[\log_{10} 99] = 1$).

(I) Find $b\_1$, $b\_{11}$, and $b\_{101}$;

(II) Find the sum of the first $1000$ terms of the sequence ${b\_n}$.

Consider the function \( f(x)=(2k-1)a^{x}-a^{-x} \) where \( a > 0 \) and \( a \neq 1 \), and it's defined as an odd function over the domain \( \mathbb{R} \).
(1) Determine the value of \( k \);
(2) If \( f(1)=- \frac{5}{6} \), the inequality \( f(3x-t)+f(-2x+1) \geq 0 \) holds for all \( x \in [-1,1] \). Find the minimum value of the real number \( t \).

A circular spinner for a game has a radius of 5 cm. The probability of winning on one spin of this spinner is $\frac{2}{5}$. What is the area, in sq cm, of the WIN sector? Express your answer in terms of $\pi$.

[asy]import graph;
draw(Circle((0,0),25),black);
draw((0,0)--(7,18),Arrow);
draw((0,0)--(0,25));
draw((0,0)--(15,-20));
label("WIN",(10,10),S);
label("LOSE",(-8,-8),N);
dot((0,0));
[/asy]

Given the function $f(x)=\cos (π+x)\cos ( \frac {3}{2}π-x)- \sqrt {3}\cos ^{2}x+ \frac{ \sqrt {3}}{2}$.
(I) Find the smallest positive period and the maximum value of $f(x)$;
(II) Find the monotonically increasing interval of $f(x)$ on $[ \frac {π}{6}, \frac {2}{3}π]$.

Given an ellipse $\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1 (a > b > 0)$ with its left and right foci being $F_1$ and $F_2$ respectively, and its eccentricity $e = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$, the length of the minor axis is $2$.  
$(1)$ Find the equation of the ellipse;  

$(2)$ Point $A$ is a moving point on the ellipse (not the endpoints of the major axis), the extension line of $AF_2$ intersects the ellipse at point $B$, and the extension line of $AO$ intersects the ellipse at point $C$. Find the maximum value of the area of $\triangle ABC$.

In the figure,  $AB \perp BC$ ,  $BC \perp CD$ , and  $BC$  is tangent to the circle with center  $O$  and diameter  $AD$ .  In which one of the following cases is the area of  $ABCD$  an integer?

[asy]
size(170);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt)+linewidth(.8pt));
pair O=origin, A=(-1/sqrt(2),1/sqrt(2)), B=(-1/sqrt(2),-1), C=(1/sqrt(2),-1), D=(1/sqrt(2),-1/sqrt(2));
draw(unitcircle);
dot(O);
draw(A--B--C--D--A);
label(" $A$ ",A,dir(A));
label(" $B$ ",B,dir(B));
label(" $C$ ",C,dir(C));
label(" $D$ ",D,dir(D));
label(" $O$ ",O,N);
[/asy] $ \textbf{(A)}\ AB=3, CD=1\qquad\textbf{(B)}\ AB=5, CD=2\qquad\textbf{(C)}\ AB=7, CD=3\qquad\textbf{(D)}\ AB=9, CD=4\qquad\textbf{(E)}\ AB=11, CD=5 $

Given three points $A$, $B$, $C$ on a straight line in the Cartesian coordinate system, satisfying $\overrightarrow{OA}=(-3,m+1)$, $\overrightarrow{OB}=(n,3)$, $\overrightarrow{OC}=(7,4)$, and $\overrightarrow{OA} \perp \overrightarrow{OB}$, where $O$ is the origin.
$(1)$ Find the values of the real numbers $m$, $n$;
$(2)$ Let $G$ be the centroid of $\triangle AOC$, and $\overrightarrow{OG}= \frac{2}{3} \overrightarrow{OB}$, find the value of $\cos \angle AOC$.

Given a sequence $\{a_n\}$ with its first $n$ terms sum $S_n$ ($n \in \mathbb{N}^*$), it is known that $a\_1=1$, $a\_2=\frac{3}{2}$, $a\_3=\frac{5}{4}$, and when $n \geqslant 2$, $4S_{n+2}+5S_n=8S_{n+1}+S_{n-1}$.

(1) Find the value of $a_4$;

(2) Prove that $\{a_{n+1}-\frac{1}{2}a_n\}$ is a geometric sequence.

Given a function $f(x)= \frac {x}{a(x+2)}$, the equation $f(x)=x$ has a unique solution. It is known that $f(x_n)=x_{n+1}$ ($n\in \mathbb{N}^*$) and $f(x_1)= \frac {2}{3}$.
(1) Prove that the sequence $\left\{ \frac {1}{x_n} \right\}$ is an arithmetic sequence;
(2) If $a_n= \frac {4-3x_n}{x_n}$ and $b_n= \frac {1}{a_na_{n+1}}$, find the sum $s_n = b_1+b_2+b_3+\ldots+b_n$;
(3) Under the conditions of (2), if the inequality $\frac {k}{\left( \frac {1}{a_1}+1 \right)\left( \frac {1}{a_2}+1 \right)\cdots \left( \frac {1}{a_n}+1 \right)}\leq \frac {1}{\sqrt {2n+1}}$ holds for all $n\in \mathbb{N}^*$, find the maximum value of $k$.

Find the number of units in the length of diagonal $DA$ of the regular hexagon shown. Express your answer in simplest radical form. [asy]
size(120);
